  "body": "## Problem and behavior\n\nThe legacy friend/vCard commands create or delete both PartyFollow directions from one caller, with no way to express consent, revisions or retry keys. Preserve their historical DTOs before enforcement, then return a non-cacheable 410 for governed relationships without changing edges or returning names. Reuse the existing canonical Follow/Connect commands for the eventual client cutover; never reinterpret reciprocal history as accepted consent.\n\nAll three routes share a transaction that locks activation, ordered accounts/credentials and the current bearer token through mutation. A committed activation, canonical pair/closure, or token revocation wins over a waiting legacy write. Pausing V2 never reopens retired commands. Partial migrations return 503.\n\n## Dependency and scope\n\n- Depends on **#402**, exact parent `8b2d1557a5e72e0346af1fd43dc7cf36033da067` (#397 \u2192 #390).\n- Implementation: `7e601607f`; production change is one shared Haskell adapter, exact existing route aliases and one additive SQL function. Most added lines are executable model/test cases and recorded evidence.\n- **Draft, unmerged; no deployment or production flag activation.** Whole-platform rollout remains blocked.\n- The audit also found `fanFollowArtist` creates reciprocal legacy edges for every fan-club member and emits notifications. That distinct integration still needs repair. Search/media/notification/delegation/moderation and native/client-cutover gaps remain tracked. This PR does not claim to close every legacy writer.\n\n## Verification actually run\n\n- TLC 1.7.2 distribution / Java 17: 9,600 generated, **4,320 distinct states**, depth 15; safety and fair progress pass. Three negative controls each violate the required authorization invariant.\n- **288 observed Commit cases** generated from the checked model and exercised through real bearer HTTP and PostgreSQL. Final harness **417 examples, 0 failures**, including activation/pair/closure/token races, competing callers, retries, no-foundation compatibility and partial-installation denial.\n- Stack / GHC 9.10.3: executable build and **2,542 tests, 0 failures**.\n- Complete-schema PostgreSQL **17.10** migration/reapply/pause and preserved-write checks pass; catalog authority audit passes.\n- Synthetic guard-only benchmark, 10,005 accounts, 40 alternating pairs per degree: protected p95 **8.06 ms** at degree 0 and **12.22 ms** at degree 10,004, below predeclared 50 ms threshold. Excludes bearer, actual mutation and HTTP costs; no production capacity claim.\n- Hosted CI for this head is running. Parent #402 social CI and [full CI35122347920](https://github.com/diegueins680/tdf-app/actions/runs/35122347920) have completed successfully at `8b2d1557a`, including backend/runtime/schema/booking checks. Its earlier pending snapshot in this source packet is superseded by the final evidence in handoff PR #394. Skipped jobs are not counted as passed.\n\n## Migration, flags and rollback\n\nApply `tdf-hq/sql/2026-09-16_social_v2_legacy_writes.sql` after the foundation, durable DM activation latch and existing adapters. It creates/replaces one function; no backfill, table rewrite, destructive down migration, automatic production migration registration, new infrastructure or flag activation.\n\nStage adapters first. Old/new binaries can coexist only in the never-activated stage without governed pair/closure state. Repair the fan-club side effect, cut over clients and drain old handlers/in-flight requests before activating existing flags. Raw database-owner writes and old binaries are outside this application adapter boundary.\n\nRollback pauses existing flags/UI or disables affected routes while retaining this adapter, the function, activation memory, canonical tombstones and historical writes. Restoring old unsafe writers or resetting activation memory is not a safe rollback. Migration and pause/reapply were verified on private synthetic databases only.\n\n## Documentation and limitations\n\nSee `docs/social/legacy-write-boundary.md` for audit, dated primary-source decisions, requirement/model/code/test traceability, exact commands, acceptance statuses and operations. `docs/social/evidence/legacy-write-boundary/` contains actual results, source fingerprints, log hashes and the corrected development failures.\n\nNo whole-app/native screenshots or production outcome improvements are claimed. Existing mobile direct Axios consumers display a generic failure for 410 until client cutover. Earlier automatic Vercel/Cloudflare deployment exceptions remain unresolved in the consolidated handoff; no deployment command was issued for this work.\n",
